From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 14:10:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 46/46] graphics: report when something reaches the display

A boot presentation needs to know when AROS has real output of its own,
so it can get out of the way -- of an error requester as readily as of a
desktop. The obvious place to look is the Display class's Show(), and it
is the wrong one. A driver may override Show() without calling
OOP_DoSuperMethod(), and this machine has one of each:
arch/aarch64-raspi's fbgfx overrides it and never forwards, while
arch/arm-native's vcgfx does not override it at all. A hook in the base
class would see half the presentations on the same machine.

display_LoadViewPorts() is above both. It is the one place where a bitmap
becomes the frontmost one, and it covers all three ways that happens --
HIDD_Display_ShowViewPorts() on a compositing driver, the software
compositor, and a plain Show() -- so nothing a driver does to its own
method table can hide a presentation from it.

What is reported is a fact, not an instruction: "something is on the
display now". graphics.library does not act on it and does not know what
the observer will do with it. The observer is optional and looked up by
name, so with nothing listening this costs one OpenResource() per view
load, and removing the observer entirely requires no change here.

Guarded by __EMU68__ because bootui.resource is this port's, and because
a target with no such resource should not pay even that.
diff --git a/rom/graphics/graphics_display.c b/rom/graphics/graphics_display.c
index a4f89d37cd..2cce06a973 100644
--- a/rom/graphics/graphics_display.c
+++ b/rom/graphics/graphics_display.c
@@ -5,6 +5,9 @@
 
 #define DEBUG 0
 #include <aros/debug.h>
+#ifdef __EMU68__
+#include <aros/bootui.h>
+#endif
 
 #include <proto/exec.h>
 #include <proto/graphics.h>
@@ -508,6 +511,31 @@ ULONG display_PrepareViewPorts(struct monitor_displaydata *mdd, struct HIDD_View
  * in it will screw up the display, likely with no chance to recover.
  * Because of this we always return zero here.
  */
+/*
+ * Report that AROS has put something on the display.
+ *
+ * This is the one place above every driver where a bitmap becomes the
+ * frontmost one, so it is where a boot presentation can learn that the system
+ * now has real output of its own -- an error requester as much as a desktop.
+ * Doing it here rather than in the generic Display class's Show() is not a
+ * preference: a driver may override Show() without calling
+ * OOP_DoSuperMethod(), and one that does would never be seen.
+ *
+ * The observer is optional and looked up by name; with nothing listening this
+ * costs one OpenResource() per view load. graphics.library states a fact and
+ * does not act on it, which keeps the dependency pointing the right way.
+ */
+static void display_ReportPresentation(struct GfxBase *GfxBase)
+{
+#ifdef __EMU68__
+    struct BootUIResource *bootui =
+        (struct BootUIResource *)OpenResource(BOOTUI_RESOURCE_NAME);
+
+    if (bootui && bootui->set_stage)
+        bootui->set_stage(BOOTUI_STAGE_PRESENTED);
+#endif
+}
+
 ULONG display_LoadViewPorts(struct monitor_displaydata *mdd, struct HIDD_ViewPortData *vpd, struct View *v, struct GfxBase *GfxBase)
 {
     struct BitMap *bitmap;
@@ -521,6 +549,8 @@ ULONG display_LoadViewPorts(struct monitor_displaydata *mdd, struct HIDD_ViewPor
     if (HIDD_Display_ShowViewPorts(mdd->mdisplay.display_obj, vpd))
     {
         DEBUG_LOADVIEW(bug("[graphics.library/display] %s: ShowViewPorts() worked\n", __func__));
+        if (vpd)
+            display_ReportPresentation(GfxBase);
         return 0;
     }
 
@@ -586,6 +616,8 @@ ULONG display_LoadViewPorts(struct monitor_displaydata *mdd, struct HIDD_ViewPor
      	 */
     	mdd->frontbm = bitmap;
 
+        display_ReportPresentation(GfxBase);
+
 	/*
 	 * Install the framebuffer into new bitmap. Only if software composition is inactive.
 	 * If it is active, our BitMap is mirrored, not replaced.
